string getCheckingQueue(string inspectorId)
{
	// THE CHECKER'S QUEUE. Custom API, POST, one argument.
	//
	// Every Plan_Item sitting at Item_Status == "Awaiting_Check", grouped by the
	// supervisor of its plan. There is no handover record between production and
	// checking - the transfer is physical, and the status IS the queue - so this
	// function is the only thing that tells the inspector work is waiting.
	//
	// WHAT inspectorId IS FOR, AND WHAT IT IS NOT FOR.
	//
	// It is validated and logged, and that is all. An empty or unknown one still
	// returns the WHOLE queue. Checking is not sliced per inspector: garments are
	// not assigned to a man, they are on a rack, and whoever is on the floor
	// checks them. Filtering by it would hide work from the only person who could
	// do it, and there are no roles or login in this app yet to make such a filter
	// mean anything - the picker is a stand-in, exactly as it is on the supervisor
	// screen.
	//
	// THE STATEMENT-EXECUTION LIMIT IS THE DESIGN CONSTRAINT HERE. It is NOT
	// catchable: it kills the script and the widget gets a bare HTTP 500 with no
	// error card, so it cannot be reported the way an ordinary failure can. The
	// naive shape - walk Plan_Item, fetch its Plan, fetch that plan's Sales_Order,
	// fetch its BOM, fetch its root line - pays for all of that once PER ITEM, and
	// a queue is many items off a handful of plans. So everything that is shared
	// is fetched once and cached in a Map keyed by id-as-string:
	//
	//   plans + their sales orders  - one fetch per PLAN, not per item
	//   the root line's figures     - one fetch per ROOT, not per batch on it
	//   BOM stage lists             - one fetch per BOM, and most items share one
	//   employee names              - one pass, for the supervisor headings
	//
	// WHY THE ORDER-LINE POSITION IS IN HERE AT ALL. Without it the inspector
	// approves 85 pieces with no idea whether that finishes the customer's line or
	// leaves it fifteen short. It is counted per ORIGINAL line - the root's
	// Qty_Ordered against the root's Qty_Accepted plus every batch hanging off it -
	// because Remake_Of always points at the ROOT, never at the previous batch.
	// That is what keeps it one flat query however many rounds it takes; Deluge has
	// no recursion.
	//
	// THE STAGE LIST IS ALWAYS THE ROOT'S BOM. Never the batch's own
	// Alteration_Stage rows. Those are what the LAST checker asked for, and
	// offering them back would narrow the choices every round until by the third
	// alteration he cannot name the stage that is actually wrong.
	supsJson = "";
	inspJson = "";

	try
	{
		//------------------------------------------------------------------
		// 0. THE INSPECTOR PICKER, AND WHO IS ASKING.
		//
		// The picker is the LOGIN STAND-IN, the same one the supervisor screen
		// uses, so it is built on every call - the widget populates it and reads
		// the queue in one round trip.
		//
		// BY ROLE, not by work. A login does not appear and disappear depending
		// on whether there is anything on the rack; an inspector with an empty
		// morning still has to sign in and look.
		//
		// Matched on the Designation TEXT. It is a plain Dropdown whose choices
		// are injected at runtime by autoPopulateDesignation from the
		// Department's Designations subform, so there is no lookup id to
		// resolve - and it follows the SPACES convention, "Quality Inspector",
		// not underscores.
		//
		// FALLS BACK TO EVERYONE IF THAT FINDS NOBODY, deliberately, and this is
		// not belt-and-braces - it is the difference between a working screen
		// and a dead one. Designation and Status are newer than most of the
		// Employee records, which are blank on both, so the moment this filter
		// ships it can legitimately match nobody - and an empty picker is a
		// widget that looks broken with nothing on screen or in the console
		// saying why. getSupervisorMaterials hit exactly this. The info line
		// below is how you tell which state you are in; once the Employee
		// records carry designations the fallback stops firing on its own.
		//------------------------------------------------------------------
		inspEmps = Employee[Designation == "Quality Inspector" && Status == "Active"] sort by Employee_Name;

		if(inspEmps.count() == 0)
		{
			info "getCheckingQueue: no Active employee has Designation 'Quality Inspector' - falling back to the full employee list. Set Designation and Status on the Employee records.";
			inspEmps = Employee[ID != 0] sort by Employee_Name;
		}

		inspTxt = ifnull(inspectorId,"").toString().trim();
		inspName = "";

		for each  ieRec in inspEmps
		{
			// Ids forced to string on BOTH sides. ID is a long and the argument
			// arrives as text; comparing them as they are is a mixed-type
			// comparison, which throws.
			if(ieRec.ID.toString() == inspTxt)
			{
				inspName = ifnull(ieRec.Employee_Name,"").toString();
			}

			if(inspJson != "")
			{
				inspJson = inspJson + ",";
			}
			// Id as a string - an 18-digit id breaks JSON.parse. Employee_Name
			// escaped: one name carrying a double quote would otherwise break
			// the parse for the whole screen, not just its own row.
			inspJson = inspJson + "{\"id\":\"" + ieRec.ID + "\",\"name\":\"" + ifnull(ieRec.Employee_Name,"").toString().replaceAll("\"","'") + "\"}";
		}

		// VALIDATED AND LOGGED, NEVER USED AS A FILTER. See the header - an
		// unknown or empty inspectorId still gets the whole queue.
		if(inspTxt == "")
		{
			info "getCheckingQueue: no inspectorId given - the full queue is returned.";
		}
		else if(inspName == "")
		{
			info "getCheckingQueue: inspectorId '" + inspTxt + "' matched nobody in the picker list - the full queue is returned anyway, deliberately.";
		}
		else
		{
			info "getCheckingQueue: inspector " + inspTxt + " (" + inspName + ")";
		}

		//------------------------------------------------------------------
		// 1. EVERY EMPLOYEE, ONCE, for the supervisor headings.
		//
		// Read as a whole rather than one lookup per plan: two plans on one
		// supervisor would otherwise fetch the same man twice, and a queue
		// spanning six plans pays six times for four names.
		//
		// NOT narrowed to supervisors. This is the directory used to NAME a man
		// on work he already has, not a picker - narrowing it would blank the
		// heading for anyone who has since changed designation.
		//------------------------------------------------------------------
		empNameById = Map();

		for each  empRec in Employee[ID != 0]
		{
			empNameById.put(empRec.ID + "",ifnull(empRec.Employee_Name,"").toString().replaceAll("\"","'"));
		}

		//------------------------------------------------------------------
		// 2. THE QUEUE ITSELF.
		//
		// "sort by" on its own assignment, never inline in the for-each header -
		// inline it does not reliably parse, and a function that will not save
		// looks exactly like one that saved and did nothing.
		//
		// Added_Time rather than Line_No: items of one plan are created together
		// and in line order, so this reads plan by plan, line by line, with
		// later batches after the originals they replace. Line_No sorted across
		// plans would interleave them.
		//------------------------------------------------------------------
		awaiting = Plan_Item[Item_Status == "Awaiting_Check"] sort by Added_Time;

		// Caches. Every one is keyed by id AS STRING - a Map keyed on a raw id
		// and read back with a string finds nothing, silently.
		planNoById = Map();
		planSoById = Map();
		planSupById = Map();

		rootOrdById = Map();
		rootAccById = Map();
		rootBomById = Map();
		rootRoundById = Map();

		bomStagesById = Map();
		skuById = Map();

		// One JSON blob per supervisor, plus the order they were first seen in.
		// A Map alone has no order worth relying on.
		supItemsById = Map();
		supOrder = List();

		for each  itm in awaiting
		{
			//----------------------------------------------------------
			// THE PLAN, AND THROUGH IT THE ORDER AND THE SUPERVISOR.
			//
			// Filled on first sight of each plan. The sales order is fetched
			// inside this block on purpose: one order produces exactly one
			// plan in this app, so per-plan already means per-order, and a
			// second cache would be two maps to keep in step for nothing.
			//----------------------------------------------------------
			planKey = ifnull(itm.Plan,"").toString().trim();

			if(planKey != "" && planNoById.get(planKey) == null)
			{
				pNoTxt = "";
				pSoTxt = "";
				pSupTxt = "";

				if(planKey.isNumber())
				{
					planId = planKey.toLong();

					for each  planRec in Production_Planning[ID == planId]
					{
						pNoTxt = ifnull(planRec.Plan_No,"").toString().replaceAll("\"","'");

						// Assigned_To is a LOOKUP, so it holds a record id, not
						// a name. The name comes off the directory above.
						pSupTxt = ifnull(planRec.Assigned_To,"").toString().trim();

						soKey = ifnull(planRec.Sales_Order,"").toString().trim();
						if(soKey != "" && soKey.isNumber())
						{
							soId = soKey.toLong();
							for each  soRec in Sales_Order[ID == soId]
							{
								pSoTxt = ifnull(soRec.Sales_Order,"").toString().replaceAll("\"","'");
							}
						}
					}
				}

				// Written even when nothing was found, so a missing plan is
				// looked up once rather than on every item hanging off it.
				planNoById.put(planKey,pNoTxt);
				planSoById.put(planKey,pSoTxt);
				planSupById.put(planKey,pSupTxt);
			}

			planNoOut = ifnull(planNoById.get(planKey),"").toString();
			soNoOut = ifnull(planSoById.get(planKey),"").toString();
			supIdTxt = ifnull(planSupById.get(planKey),"").toString();

			//----------------------------------------------------------
			// THE ROOT LINE.
			//
			// Remake_Of when it is set, the item's own id otherwise. A Creator
			// field never written is EMPTY, not null, and ifnull() does not
			// catch empty - so it is normalised to text and tested for "",
			// never for null.
			//
			// Read WITHOUT consulting Is_Remake, unlike recheckOrderComplete.
			// It only decides which line's figures to show, so a batch with no
			// Remake_Of - the orphan state recheckOrderComplete warns about -
			// falls back to reading its own position rather than showing the
			// inspector nothing. Nothing is written here, so the worst case is
			// a number that under-reports, not one that corrupts a line.
			//----------------------------------------------------------
			rootKey = itm.ID.toString();
			rmOfTxt = ifnull(itm.Remake_Of,"").toString().trim();
			if(rmOfTxt != "")
			{
				rootKey = rmOfTxt;
			}

			if(rootOrdById.get(rootKey) == null)
			{
				rOrdTxt = "0";
				rAccNum = 0.0;
				rBomTxt = "";
				rRounds = 0;
				lineIds = List();

				if(rootKey.isNumber())
				{
					rootId = rootKey.toLong();
					lineIds.add(rootId);

					for each  rootRec in Plan_Item[ID == rootId]
					{
						// Every numeric normalised before it is read as one.
						// toDecimal() THROWS on an empty field, and these are
						// exactly the fields that stay empty until the first
						// check writes them.
						rOrdTxt = ifnull(rootRec.Qty_Ordered,"0").toString().trim();
						if(rOrdTxt == "")
						{
							rOrdTxt = "0";
						}

						rAccTxt = ifnull(rootRec.Qty_Accepted,"0").toString().trim();
						if(rAccTxt == "")
						{
							rAccTxt = "0";
						}
						rAccNum = rAccNum + rAccTxt.toDecimal();

						// BOM is copied onto every batch, but the ROOT's is the
						// one the alteration picker must offer - see the header.
						rBomTxt = ifnull(rootRec.BOM,"").toString().trim();
					}

					// ONE FLAT QUERY for the whole line. Remake_Of points at the
					// root from every generation of batch, so this catches the
					// third alteration of a remake without any recursion.
					for each  kidRec in Plan_Item[Remake_Of == rootId]
					{
						kAccTxt = ifnull(kidRec.Qty_Accepted,"0").toString().trim();
						if(kAccTxt == "")
						{
							kAccTxt = "0";
						}
						rAccNum = rAccNum + kAccTxt.toDecimal();

						lineIds.add(kidRec.ID);
					}

					//------------------------------------------------
					// WHICH ROUND THIS IS: how many checks the line has
					// already had, plus one.
					//
					// Counted per line member rather than by scanning
					// Item_Check whole. That table only grows - a year in it
					// is every check the factory has ever done - and reading
					// all of it to answer a question about one line is the
					// shape that walks into the statement limit later, when
					// there is live data and no way to test it.
					//
					// The queue is small by construction: it is what is on
					// the rack right now, and a line carries a handful of
					// batches at most.
					//
					// .count() and not .size() - size() is a List method, a
					// query result answers to count().
					//------------------------------------------------
					for each  lineKey in lineIds
					{
						chkRows = Item_Check[Plan_Item == lineKey];
						rRounds = rRounds + chkRows.count();
					}
				}

				rootOrdById.put(rootKey,rOrdTxt);
				rootAccById.put(rootKey,rAccNum.toString());
				rootBomById.put(rootKey,rBomTxt);
				rootRoundById.put(rootKey,(rRounds + 1).toString());
			}

			//----------------------------------------------------------
			// THE ROOT'S STAGE LIST, cached per BOM.
			//
			// Many items share one BOM - every size of one style does - so
			// this is fetched once per BOM rather than once per item.
			//----------------------------------------------------------
			bomKey = ifnull(rootBomById.get(rootKey),"").toString();

			if(bomKey != "" && bomStagesById.get(bomKey) == null)
			{
				stgJson = "";

				if(bomKey.isNumber())
				{
					bomId = bomKey.toLong();

					for each  bomRec in BOM[ID == bomId]
					{
						for each  bstg in bomRec.Production_Stages
						{
							seqTxt = ifnull(bstg.Sequence_No,"0").toString().trim();
							if(seqTxt == "")
							{
								seqTxt = "0";
							}

							// Stage names are free text on the BOM until the
							// stage master lands, so they are escaped like any
							// other free text.
							opTxt = ifnull(bstg.Operation,"").toString().trim().replaceAll("\"","'");

							if(opTxt != "")
							{
								if(stgJson != "")
								{
									stgJson = stgJson + ",";
								}
								stgJson = stgJson + "{\"sequence\":" + seqTxt.toDecimal().toLong() + ",\"operation\":\"" + opTxt + "\"}";
							}
						}
					}
				}

				bomStagesById.put(bomKey,stgJson);
			}

			stagesOut = ifnull(bomStagesById.get(bomKey),"").toString();

			//----------------------------------------------------------
			// THE SKU.
			//
			// Item_Sku is a LOOKUP to Item_Master, so the field holds a record
			// id. Sending it raw put an 18-digit id on screen where the code
			// the floor recognises should be, twice before.
			//----------------------------------------------------------
			skuKey = ifnull(itm.Item_Sku,"").toString().trim();

			if(skuKey != "" && skuById.get(skuKey) == null)
			{
				skuTxt = "";

				if(skuKey.isNumber())
				{
					skuId = skuKey.toLong();
					for each  imRec in Item_Master[ID == skuId]
					{
						skuTxt = ifnull(imRec.SKU,"").toString().replaceAll("\"","'");
					}
				}

				skuById.put(skuKey,skuTxt);
			}

			skuOut = ifnull(skuById.get(skuKey),"").toString();

			//----------------------------------------------------------
			// WHICH KIND OF BATCH THIS IS.
			//
			// Is_Remake reads EMPTY, not false, on anything planned before the
			// field existed - so the ifnull default is what makes those old
			// records read as originals, which is what they are.
			//
			// FOUR KINDS, NOT THREE, and Production_Loss is the one that must
			// not be folded in with the rest. It replaces pieces spoiled on the
			// floor - garments that were NEVER inspected and never rejected.
			// Drawing it as a rejection tells the inspector these failed his own
			// check when they never reached him, and quietly moves the blame for
			// a cutting-table accident onto his own last round.
			//
			// production.js already draws this distinction on the supervisor's
			// screen ("Replacement batch" against "QC remake"), so collapsing it
			// here would also make the two screens disagree about one item.
			//
			// Check_Reject is the rejection spelling. Empty reads as a rejection
			// too - every remake raised before the field existed came from one.
			//----------------------------------------------------------
			batchTxt = "Original";

			if(ifnull(itm.Is_Remake,false) == true)
			{
				rsnTxt = ifnull(itm.Remake_Reason,"").toString().trim();

				if(rsnTxt == "Alteration")
				{
					batchTxt = "Alteration";
				}
				else if(rsnTxt == "Production_Loss")
				{
					batchTxt = "Replacement";
				}
				else
				{
					batchTxt = "Remake";
				}
			}

			//----------------------------------------------------------
			// The figures, all normalised through text first.
			//
			// Qty_Produced is what production says it made, and it is what the
			// check has to be split three ways against. An empty field emitted
			// straight into JSON produces "produced": with nothing after it,
			// which breaks JSON.parse for the entire screen rather than for
			// one row.
			//----------------------------------------------------------
			prodTxt = ifnull(itm.Qty_Produced,"0").toString().trim();
			if(prodTxt == "")
			{
				prodTxt = "0";
			}
			producedOut = prodTxt.toDecimal().toLong();

			ordTxt = ifnull(rootOrdById.get(rootKey),"0").toString().trim();
			if(ordTxt == "")
			{
				ordTxt = "0";
			}
			accTxt = ifnull(rootAccById.get(rootKey),"0").toString().trim();
			if(accTxt == "")
			{
				accTxt = "0";
			}

			ordOut = ordTxt.toDecimal().toLong();
			accOut = accTxt.toDecimal().toLong();

			// CLAMPED AT 0. Acceptances can legitimately pass the ordered
			// quantity - a batch is sized to what was rejected, and nothing
			// stops a checker accepting all of it after some of the original
			// was already accepted - and a negative "still to come" reads as an
			// arithmetic bug on screen rather than as a line that is finished.
			outOut = ordOut - accOut;
			if(outOut < 0)
			{
				outOut = 0;
			}

			roundTxt = ifnull(rootRoundById.get(rootKey),"1").toString().trim();
			if(roundTxt == "")
			{
				roundTxt = "1";
			}
			roundOut = roundTxt.toDecimal().toLong();

			//----------------------------------------------------------
			// Into its supervisor's bucket.
			//
			// An item whose plan has no supervisor still appears, under an
			// empty id. Dropping it would hide real work on the rack because
			// of a blank field on a record nobody is looking at.
			//----------------------------------------------------------
			if(supItemsById.get(supIdTxt) == null)
			{
				supItemsById.put(supIdTxt,"");
				supOrder.add(supIdTxt);
			}

			itemsJson = supItemsById.get(supIdTxt).toString();
			if(itemsJson != "")
			{
				itemsJson = itemsJson + ",";
			}

			// Every id a STRING - an 18-digit id breaks JSON.parse on the
			// widget side. Every quantity a bare number.
			itemsJson = itemsJson + "{\"id\":\"" + itm.ID + "\",\"name\":\"" + ifnull(itm.Item_Name,"").toString().replaceAll("\"","'") + "\",\"sku\":\"" + skuOut + "\",\"planId\":\"" + planKey + "\",\"planNo\":\"" + planNoOut + "\",\"salesOrder\":\"" + soNoOut + "\",\"produced\":" + producedOut + ",\"round\":" + roundOut + ",\"batch\":\"" + batchTxt + "\",\"lineOrdered\":" + ordOut + ",\"lineAccepted\":" + accOut + ",\"lineOutstanding\":" + outOut + ",\"stages\":[" + stagesOut + "]}";

			supItemsById.put(supIdTxt,itemsJson);
		}

		//------------------------------------------------------------------
		// 3. THE ACCORDION.
		//
		// Only supervisors who actually have something waiting. Listing every
		// supervisor here means opening a heading and finding nothing, with
		// nothing on screen to say why.
		//------------------------------------------------------------------
		for each  supKey in supOrder
		{
			supName = "Unassigned";

			if(supKey != "")
			{
				nameTxt = ifnull(empNameById.get(supKey),"").toString();
				if(nameTxt != "")
				{
					supName = nameTxt;
				}
				else
				{
					// Named by id rather than left blank. A supervisor whose
					// Employee record has gone still has garments on the rack,
					// and a heading reading nothing is unattributable.
					supName = "Employee " + supKey;
				}
			}

			if(supsJson != "")
			{
				supsJson = supsJson + ",";
			}
			supsJson = supsJson + "{\"id\":\"" + supKey + "\",\"name\":\"" + supName + "\",\"items\":[" + ifnull(supItemsById.get(supKey),"").toString() + "]}";
		}
	}
	catch (e)
	{
		// Creator collapses every runtime failure reaching a widget into "code
		// 9430", which says nothing at all. The real message travels back inside
		// the payload, in the shape the widget already parses.
		// The picker goes back too when it was already built - the widget can
		// then still show who it thinks you are while reporting the failure.
		return "{\"errors\":[\"DELUGE: " + e.toString().replaceAll("\"","'") + "\"],\"inspectors\":[" + inspJson + "],\"supervisors\":[]}";
	}

	// Built by hand. Map.toString() does not emit valid JSON once the structure
	// nests, and List.toString() does not wrap in [].
	return "{\"errors\":[],\"inspectors\":[" + inspJson + "],\"supervisors\":[" + supsJson + "]}";
}
